Greetings.

After seeing Gerald Jean's speed improvements yesterday, I've been
suffering from fits of jealousy.  I'm running some straightforward
Monte Carlo simulations, but they are painfully slow because I can't get
my brain to step outside of its rut with a for (or For) loop.  I'm
new to function writing and programming so please bear with me. 
I've figured out enough to pull the sampling out of the loop, which of
course speeds things considerably.  That's as far as I can get, and
I know there are better ways.  Any help would be much
appreciated.

Basically what I want to do is "draw" a bunch of normal forms
with randomly sampled parameter values, and then take vertical slices at
known points to examine the density.  My primitive code
follows.  

my.points <- seq(-2, 10, 0.1)  #vector of points over which to
calculate curve

mu <- sample(density(my.data1, n=1000, na.rm=T)$x, 

        prob=density(my.data1,
n=1000, na.rm=T)$y, replace=T, size=10000) #random sample of mus

sigma <- sample(density(my.data2, n=1000, na.rm=T)$x, 

        prob=density(my.data2,
n=1000, na.rm=T)$y, replace=T, size=10000)  #random sample of
sigmas

#"draw" curves, get intersection points, and store result

For(i=1:10000, {

        tmp <-
(1/(sigma[i] * sqrt(2*pi))) * (exp(-0.5 * ((my.point - mu[i]) /
sigma[i])^2))

        output.values[i,1]
<- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=3)$y

        output.values[i,2]
<- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=4)$y

        output.values[i,3]
<- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=5)$y

        output.values[i,4]
<- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=6)$y

        output.values[i,5]
<- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=7)$y

})

densityplot(~output.values[,1])

Thank you!

Todd Hatfield

