From hatfield at solander.bc.ca  Fri Mar  2 04:35:02 2001
From: hatfield at solander.bc.ca (Todd Hatfield)
Date: Thu, 01 Mar 2001 20:35:02 -0800
Subject: summary: avoiding a loop
Message-ID: <5.0.2.1.0.20010301201347.00ab40c0@solander.bc.ca>

Thank you very much to Bill Dunlap, Tim Hesterberg, and Jingshan Zhang for 
their help with my looping problem.  Bill showed me several ways to make 
the loop more efficient, and Jingshan pointed out the dnorm() 
function.  The real gem was learning from Tim how to use outer(), which 
sped up calculation by more than an order of magnitude.  They all caught a 
mis-spelling of "my.points" in the code I submitted.

Thank you all.

Sincerely, Todd Hatfield

Original (corrected) post and abbreviated replies below.

Greetings.

After seeing Gerald Jean's speed improvements yesterday, I've been 
suffering from fits of jealousy. I'm running some straightforward Monte 
Carlo simulations, but they are painfully slow because I can't get my brain 
to step outside of its rut with a for (or For) loop. I'm new to function 
writing and programming so please bear with me. I've figured out enough to 
pull the sampling out of the loop, which of course speeds things 
considerably. That's as far as I can get, and I know there are better ways. 
Any help would be much appreciated.

Basically what I want to do is "draw" a bunch of normal forms with randomly 
sampled parameter values, and then take vertical slices at known points to 
examine the density. My primitive code follows.

my.points <- seq(-2, 10, 0.1) #vector of points over which to calculate curve

mu <- sample(density(my.data1, n=1000, na.rm=T)$x,

prob=density(my.data1, n=1000, na.rm=T)$y, replace=T, size=10000) #random 
sample of mus

sigma <- sample(density(my.data2, n=1000, na.rm=T)$x,

prob=density(my.data2, n=1000, na.rm=T)$y, replace=T, size=10000) #random 
sample of sigmas

#"draw" curves, get intersection points, and store result

For(i=1:10000, {

tmp <- (1/(sigma[i] * sqrt(2*pi))) * (exp(-0.5 * ((my.points - mu[i]) / 
sigma[i])^2))

output.values[i,1] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=3)$y

output.values[i,2] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=4)$y

output.values[i,3] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=5)$y

output.values[i,4] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=6)$y

output.values[i,5] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=7)$y

})

densityplot(~output.values[,1])

Thank you!
Todd Hatfield

>From Bill Dunlap:
Have you tried this as a function that uses for(i in 1:10000)...
instead of as a For loop? For() can save memory but is generally
much slower than for (because it does a lot more reading and writing
of the disk). Doing things in a function cuts down on
the overhead involved in preparing to save every object to disk.
> For(i=1:10000, {
> tmp <- (1/(sigma[i] * sqrt(2*pi))) * (exp(-0.5 * ((my.point - mu[i]) /
> sigma[i])^2))
> output.values[i,1] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=3)$y
> output.values[i,2] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=4)$y
> output.values[i,3] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=5)$y
> output.values[i,4] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=6)$y
> output.values[i,5] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=7)$y
Those last 5 lines could be replaced by
output.values[i,1;5] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=3:7)$y
and that should cut the looping time down by a factor of about 5.
(The 1:5 could be left out if ncol(output.values)==5.)

>From Tim Hesterberg:

>my.points <- seq(-2, 10, 0.1) #vector of points over which to calculate 
curve

>mu <- sample(density(my.data1, n=1000, na.rm=T)$x,

> prob=density(my.data1, n=1000, na.rm=T)$y, replace=T, size=10000)

You don't need to call density() twice. Instead:

temp <- density(my.data1, n=1000, na.rm=T)

>mu <- sample(temp$x, prob=temp$y, replace=T, size=10000)

>#"draw" curves, get intersection points, and store result
>For(i=1:10000, {
> tmp <- (1/(sigma[i] * sqrt(2*pi))) * (exp(-0.5 * ((my.point -
>mu[i]) / sigma[i])^2))
Is that my.point or my.points?

If you meant the vector of my.points, then you can do the calculation
with outer(). Need to be a big clever, because one dimension
corresponds to my.points and the other to both mu and sigma.
Try something like this:
outer(my.points, 1:length(mu),
function(x, ivec, mu, sigma) dnorm(x, mean=mu[ivec], sd=sigma[ivec]),
mu = mu, sigma = sigma)
> output.values[i,1] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=3)$y
> output.values[i,2] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=4)$y
> output.values[i,3] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=5)$y
> output.values[i,4] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=6)$y
> output.values[i,5] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=7)$y
Again, you don't need to repeat all that. If you keep it in the
loop try:
output.values[i,] <- approx(my.points, tmp, xout=3:7)$y

Better yet, use outer() instead of using the loop.
Caveat -- I haven't actually tried any of this, because I'm not
sure exactly what you mean to do.
The basic idea is to try to avoid loops by operating on whole
objects at a time, as vectors, or to use outer() to produce
a matrix result corresponding to vectors for the two dimensions.
See help(outer)
It may also help to try it with simpler calculations first,
e.g. if you have just mu, with constant sigma = 5, then
outer(my.points, mu, dnorm, sd = 5)

