From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Sun Mar  4 22:54:54 2001
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Sun, 04 Mar 2001 22:54:54 +0000
Subject: Re: ARIMA sigma2 & coef std err
In-Reply-To: <4.3.0.20010304150522.00a98ac0@saluki-mail.siu.edu>
References: <4.3.0.20010304150522.00a98ac0@saluki-mail.siu.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.31.0103042247160.24126-100000@auk.stats>

On Sun, 4 Mar 2001, Wayne Thogmartin wrote:

> I guess I have 2 questions.
>
> Q1: When calculating an ARIMA, you can return SIGMA2, which is the
> estimated innovations variance.  I'm not familiar with the term
> "innovations" in this context, but I take this to mean the estimated
> residual variance, which is the reduced sums of squares divided by sample
> size minus the number of model parameters.  Is this correct?

The innovations are the independent `errors' in the ARMA model.  They are
not residuals, since with an MA part present, this is a not a regression.

As far as I know, if you are using arima.mle, it maximizes a conditional
likelihood, so the component sigma2 is the (conditional) mle.  So even for
an AR process, it has a different divisor than that you mention.

> Q2: How might I calculate standard errors of the ARIMA coefficients?

>From component var.coef.

You might find it helpful to look at the worked examples in Venables &
Ripley (1999, chapter 13).

