From bert_gunter at merck.com  Tue Mar  6 13:44:12 2001
From: bert_gunter at merck.com (Gunter, Bert)
Date: Tue, 06 Mar 2001 08:44:12 -0500
Subject: Editorial comments on Tim Hesterberg's "improved" subsets()
Message-ID: <994DC7B72E4CD311A56D0008C707B359037CED33@usrymx05.merck.com>

Hi all:

Tim Hesterberg's version of the subsets() function prompts me to note the
following, which I hope others may find useful:

1) S is a full computer language tuned for data analysis tasks, not merely a
command processor, and this is a good example of the flexibility of
implementation that this power affords.

2) Any recursive function can **always** be written iteratively and
vice-versa. So the major issues are:
a) speed for the size of problem for which the code is intended ; b) ease of
programming;  c)readability and maintainability of the code

I think that without question, the recursive implementation is clearly the
winner on b) and c), since slick indexing schemes are not required. However,
it is equally clear that in S (though maybe not in other languages, like
LISP-STAT) the recursive implementation is much slower for large problems.
However, in small problems, it is completely competitive and adequate, and
so may be preferable. I believe that, in fact, in most circumstances where
one would want to find all possible combinations of (n,k), n and k are
probably small (because otherwise the answer is ridiculously large anyway).
So my guess is that the Ripley/Venables code is still preferable for most
applications. Obviously, having Hesterberg's version on hand for larger
problems is helpful.

All in all, a good lesson in both programming and the power of the S
language.

Cheers,
