I would like to thank Jingshan Zhang, Dennis Murphy, Samuel Buttrey, Jean
Adams, James Holtman, Greg Snow, Shawn Boles, Steven McKinney, and Gerry
Blais for their very helpful solutions to my problem [original message is
below].  In my post, I had neglected to inform readers that the ASCII files
I was working with were not entirely in tabular form and that there were
different numbers of columns in different parts of the file.  Furthermore,
sometimes entire rows were character.

I modified and merged some of the contributors' solutions to arrive at the
one I used.  A copy is shown below.

For (i in 1:199){
mc <- scan(file = paste('data.',i,'.out', sep=''), what = list(' '),
                skip = 255, sep = '\n')[[1]]
mc.p <- substring(mc,5,5)=='X' #desired character is in column 5
a <- matrix(which(mc.p==1),,1) #row number with desired information
#create data frame with information from select columns
mc.x <- cbind.data.frame(v1=substring(mc[mc.p],24,30), v2=
substring(mc[mc.p],37,43), v3 = substring(mc[mc.p],50,56),
                        v4 = substring(mc[mc.p],63,69), v5 = 
substring(mc[mc.p],72,77),v6=a)
#change mode of data
mc.dat <- matrix(as.numeric(unlist(mc.x)),2,6)
}

