Subject: analysis of proportions data
Message-ID: <409B678FC2558E4F925766483302C811039494@BUS1.bus.olemiss.edu>

This question relates to how to analyze proportions data, and given that
I am correct in how to analyze it, what is the right way to implement it
in Splus. 
 
I have a data set that looks something like this
 
unit       year   succ  total   x
1           1980   10     50      5
1           1990   12     48      6
1           2000   20     60      4
2           1980   40     60      3
2           1990   12     30      2
2           2000   10     20      4
 
The way to interpret this is that in unit 1 in 1980 there were 50
experiments, 10 lead to a success, and the remaining 40 a failure.   The
question is to what extend the variable x explains the likelihood of a
success.
 
I could not find any problems like this discussed in Splus, so I looked
at STATA.  The way the STATA manual suggests to solve this problem is by
running a logit and using the option "frequency weight."   My
understanding of what the frequency weight function does is as follows.
In the first line the weight would be 50 and Stata would treat this as
if the first line was replaced by 10 lines that look like this
 
1           1980   1      5
 
(where "1" is a success)
 
and 40 lines that look like that
 
1           1980   0     5
 
(where "0" stand for failure).
 
Then, STATA runs a standard logit regression with this expanded data
set.
 
Here are my questions.
 
1.  Is this what I should do in Splus?   I.e., manually expand my data
set
(with all these duplications) and then run a logit?    My only concern
is
that my data set would then be quite big: (94 units) x (30 years) x (800
observations per unit/year) so a bit over 2 million lines.
2. Alternatively, I can keep the data in the original format.  If one
assumes
that the individual observations are derived from a logit model, one can
write down the maximum likelihood funciton in terms of the orginal data
(successes, and total observations).  If this is the way to go, I am
sure I can figure out how to do that, but if someone has written a
function that will compute such a maximum likelihood (i.e., written
down the gradient, hessian, and so on), it will save me some time.
 
Richard
 

From rboylan at bus.olemiss.edu  Sat Mar 10 20:09:14 2001
From: rboylan at bus.olemiss.edu (Boylan, Richard)
Date: Sat, 10 Mar 2001 14:09:14 -0600
Subject: 
Message-ID: <409B678FC2558E4F925766483302C811039499@BUS1.bus.olemiss.edu>

  

This is a summary to my earlier question on the analysis of proportion
data. Thanks for all the people who sent me emails.

 

I  am including my post, an answer, and my comment to the answer.
Thanks for everyone who sent me emails.

 

 I have a data set that looks something like this

 unit       year   succ  total   x
 1           1980   10     50      5
 1           1990   12     48      6
 1           2000   20     60      4
 2           1980   40     60      3
 2           1990   12     30      2
 2           2000   10     20      4

 The way to interpret this is that in unit 1 in 1980 there were 50
 experiments, 10 lead to a success, and the remaining 40 a failure.
The
 question is to what extend the variable x explains the likelihood of a
 success.  

 The question related how to analyze the effect of x on the probability
of a success.

Several people suggested running a GLM, here, I am copying Renaud
Lancelot’s answer which was the most complete.

 test <- read.table(file = "clipboard", col.names = c("unit", "year",
"succ", "total", "x"))
 test
  unit year succ total x
1    1 1980   10    50 5
2    1 1990   12    48 6
3    1 2000   20    60 4
4    2 1980   40    60 3
5    2 1990   12    30 2
6    2 2000   10    20 4
 summary(glm(succ/total ~ x, data = test, weights = total, family =
binomial(logit)))

Call: glm(formula = succ/total ~ x, family = binomial(logit), data =
test, weights = total)
Deviance Residuals:
         1         2          3        4        5         6
 -1.543096 0.5606936 -0.9167769 2.697383 -2.18573 0.9915267

