From: jl7001 at axe.humboldt.edu (Jack Lewis)
Date: Mon, 12 Mar 2001 12:20:37 -0800
Subject: Mixed model for a multiple-watershed study
Message-ID: <3AAD2F95.F46E61E2@axe.humboldt.edu>

I am trying to formulate a mixed model applicable to multiple-watershed
experiments.  (This is an important topic in hydrological research that
has never been adequately addressed).  The design involves measurements
of a response variable such as storm peak flows at multiple
stream-gaging stations before and after a land management treatment is
applied.  A control watershed is left untreated for the entire period of
study.  A number of storms occurs before and after treatment.  The
response to storms at the control watershed is a continuous covariate
and I would like to model the variation among gaging stations as a
random effect.  I believe storm might be viewed as a random blocking
factor.  (Antecedent conditions and storm characteristics such as
rainfall intensity and duration tend to make responses within a storm
more uniform than responses between storms of a given rainfall amount.)
I also know that the errors for nearby stations are positively
correlated and I would initially like to treat consecutive storms as
independent.

The basic model looks like this

y_ij = alpha + beta * x_j + a_i  + b_i * x_j + other fixed effects +
random error

where y_ij is the response of watershed i in storm j
x_j is the response at the control watershed in storm j
alpha and beta are fixed effects for the mean response
a_i and b_i are random effects for watershed i

AND the covariances of the random errors are modelled as a function of
spatial separation between watersheds.  The error covariance matrix
might be viewed as a block diagonal structure with an identical diagonal
block for each storm.

I am having trouble seeing how to do this with the nlme package.  I know
it has the capability to model within-group covariance structures.  But
I want to model the between-watershed (within-storm) covariances, not
the within-watershed covariances.  How can such a model be specified for
nlme?

Thank you very much for considering this problem.

