From Nancy.Lo at noaa.gov  Tue Mar 13 22:22:25 2001
From: Nancy.Lo at noaa.gov (Nancy Lo)
Date: Tue, 13 Mar 2001 14:22:25 -0800
Subject: Summary of se of predicted values of GAM
Message-ID: <3AAE9DA0.8B4B21ED@noaa.gov>

Hi all,

I THANK  Peter England and John Leathwick for taking time to provide me
quite different and
interesting approaches to  get se of the  predicted  values from GAM on
a new data se.

Peter?s method is to include the new data set with the old one, and fit
the gam model with this
new data set, but weighting out the new observations, you should be able
to get fitted values and
standard errors for the whole lot. By weighting out the new
observations, he meant to assign
weights of 0 value to the new data points, so the fitting does not
include the new data set.
However, you should be able to get predicted values and standard errors
of all variables in the
data set, regardless of weight. Obviously, you will need to create a
suitable vector of weights.

John Leathwick has two approaches and he gave an example for his first
approaches.

John stated:..., ?We have used two approaches used. One we have played
with is to
extract the standard errors for the original data set using predict.gam,
and then model their
distribution in  relation to the response variables using a gam with
gaussian errors - this can then
be used to predict the  distribution of errors in the prediction space.
Rather pragmatic, and I'm not 100% satisfied as to its validity, but
its very illuminating.

The second alternative is to extract the values of both the smooth
functions and their standard
errors, and use  the latter to manually calculate the errors for a new
set of predictions - a
colleague, Anthony Lehmann, has more   experience with this, and I have
copied this e-mail to
him - he may be able to give you more detailed advice on  this option. ?

An example of his first approach.

 tree.gam <-  gam(nz.tree$daccup ~ s(mat) + s(mas) + s(soil.deficit) +
s(slope) +
 lith + drain, data = nz.site, binomial) predset <-
 predict.gam(tree.gam,nz.site,"response",se=T) se.gam <- gam(predset$se
 ~ s(mat) + s(mas) + s(soil.deficit) + s(slope) + lith + drain, data =
 nz.site, gaussian) (you would need to check the name of the standard
 errors in the prediction data set (predset) with names(predset) - I
 don't have one handy I can check)
new.ses <-  predict.gam(se.gam,newpredictors,"response")

