I knocked this function up recently to solve a similar problem: converting
the output of by() into a data frame.  I've hardly tested it, but it seems
to work when the result is a vector or named list of scalars (basically,
anything that could be converted to a data frame).

as.data.frame.by <- function(byobj) {
## works when result of by is a vector, or list of scalars
## basically works by rbinding together all the cells of byobj
        dn <- dimnames(byobj)
        combs <- expand.grid(lapply(dn,factor))
        rbind.args <- as.list(byobj)
        rbind.args[[1]] <- data.frame(rbind.args[[1]])
        valdf <- do.call("rbind", rbind.args)
        cbind(combs, valdf)
}

Use at your own risk, usual disclaimers apply, etc.

# David

