From a296180 at mica.fmr.com  Fri Mar 16 17:12:50 2001
From: a296180 at mica.fmr.com (David Kane - GEODE <David Kane)
Date: Fri, 16 Mar 2001 12:12:50 -0500
Subject: Summary of Woorking with objects of class "by"
Message-ID: <15026.14674.373177.33099@arbres2a>

Thanks to Matthew D. Austin, James Holtman, Ed Kademan, Don MacQueen, Marcel
Baumgartner, Patrick Connolly, Ray Brownrigg, Nick Ellis, David Siev, Dave
Lorenz and David Smith for taking the time to answer. The generosity of the S+
community is truly impressive.

In this message, you will find:

1) The answer that I choose.
2) Some warnings and weirdness that came up in the discussion
3) Other thoughts from the respondants.

Apologies in advance for any misunderstandings or misattributions.

Recall the problem:

#######################
> version
Version 6.0 Release 1 for Sun SPARC, SunOS 5.6 : 2000 
> y <- data.frame(a = 
> as.factor(c("level1","level1","level2","level2","level2")), b = c(1:5))
> y
       a b 
1 level1 1
2 level1 2
3 level2 3
4 level2 4
5 level2 5
> byObject <- by(y$b, y$a, function(x) {sum(x) + x^2})
> byObject
INDICES:level1
  x 
1 4
2 7
----------------------------------------------------------
INDICES:level2
   x 
3 21
4 28
5 37

#################################

The key issue is that I want to manipulate the result of the "by" command into
something, like a dataframe, easier to work with. The reason that I am using
"by" -- as opposed to tapply -- is that the functions that I want to apply to
each level of a factor generally take dataframes as input arguments.

1) My (current) solution is to follow James Holtman's advice and use:

> tempDF <- NULL
> for (i in seq(dim(byObject))) tempDF <- rbind(tempDF,byObject[[i]])
> tempDF
   x 
1  4
2  7
3 21
4 28
5 37
> 

This does the trick and is fast enough for me. One addition is that I change
the definition of my function to return a dataframe with row.names matching the
row names of the input dataset. This allows me to keep track of what input
observations are associated with which outputs fairly easily. Note that you
must initialize (if that's the right word) tempDF before starting the loop, or
this will fail.

2) A couple items of weirdness and warning came up during the process. Several
people suggested making use of the "unlist" command. For example,

> unlist(byObject)
[1]  4  7 21 28 37

Given this, why not just write:

> y$x <- unlist(byObject)
> y
       a b  x 
1 level1 1  4
2 level1 2  7
3 level2 3 21
4 level2 4 28
5 level2 5 37
> 

The problem, of course, is that this assumes too much about the order of the
factors in the input dataset. It would not work if, for example, the dataset
were:
> y <- data.frame(a = 
> as.factor(c("level2","level1","level1","level2","level2")), b = c(3,1,2,4,5))
> y
       a b 
1 level2 3
2 level1 1
3 level1 2
4 level2 4
5 level2 5
> byObject <- by(y$b, y$a, function(x) {sum(x) + x^2})
> y$x <- unlist(byObject)
> y
       a b  x 
1 level2 3  4               *WRONG ANSWER*
2 level1 1  7
3 level1 2 21
4 level2 4 28
5 level2 5 37

There are ways around this problem (see below), but this seemed somewhat
dangerous to me. For me, the best approach turned out to be to ensure that
input and output dataframes have appropriate row names so that I don't have to
worry about the ordering of dataframes and what the called (by "by") function
might or might not do to them.

One weirdness that came up was the names attribute for byObject. For me, that's
NULL.

> names(byObject)
NULL

Apparently, earlier versions of S+ would provide the names of the factor as the
names of the items in the array. Several respondents made use of this
"feature".

3) Other thoughts (not sent to the list) included:

a) David Smith has a function:

as.data.frame.by <- function(byobj) {
## works when result of by is a vector, or list of scalars
## basically works by rbinding together all the cells of byobj
        dn <- dimnames(byobj)
        combs <- expand.grid(lapply(dn,factor))
        rbind.args <- as.list(byobj)
        rbind.args[[1]] <- data.frame(rbind.args[[1]])
        valdf <- do.call("rbind", rbind.args)
        cbind(combs, valdf)
}

which, alas, failed for me. I am not sure why?

b) Several people pointed out Dr. Harrell's Hmisc library, especially the
summarize function. This is an impressive body of work, but I wanted to
manipulate the objects myself.

c) Ed Kademan suggested:

> y$x <- numeric(nrow(y))
> k <- as.numeric(unlist(lapply(byObject, function(x) dimnames(x)[[1]])))
> y[k,'x'] <- unlist(byObject)
> y
       a b  x 
1 level2 3 21
2 level1 1  4
3 level1 2  7
4 level2 4 28
5 level2 5 37

d) Patrick Connolly pointed out that the following would work fine:

> y <- data.frame(a = 
> as.factor(c("level2","level1","level1","level2","level2")), b = c(3,1,2,4,5))
> y
       a b 
1 level2 3
2 level1 1
3 level1 2
4 level2 4
5 level2 5
> yy <- y[order(y$a),]
> yy$x <- unlist(by(y$b, y$a, function(x) {sum(x) + x^2}))
> yy[row.names(y),]
       a b  x 
1 level2 3 21
2 level1 1  4
3 level1 2  7
4 level2 4 28
5 level2 5 37

Thanks again to everyone for taking the time and effort to help me with my
problem.

Dave Kane

