Subject: summary: beta-binomial regression (2nd edition)
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.10103192148280.20762-100000@eureka.stern.nyu.edu>

Hi S+ers,
Private messages to me have made it clear that my summary of the
beta-binomial regression results was not clear enough. Let me try again:

(1) "Look at the Venables and Ripley functions": As far as I know, there
    are no functions to adapt glm to beta-binomial regression. There is,
    however, one for the negative binomial (actually, two). The suggestion
    was to mimic the way those functions were written to get one for the
    beta-binomial.

(2) "Look at the material on Pat Altham's web site": in fact, these
    worksheets and slides only discuss the beta-binomial model without
    covariates. To fit a beta-binomial regression would require modifying
    the log-likelihood to include covariates.

Thus, as far as I know, there is (still) no "out of the box" function for
fitting beta-binomial regression, in the sense of "family=beta.binomial"
for glm. Any volunteers?

                  Best,

                  Jeff

