Message-ID: <001e01c0b0ea$7ad09620$dff33f92@joelr.state.ak.us>

Hi,
it is small, but will save someone 1/2 a day. attached (and inserted below)
are the scripts and a help file.  Note this is a revision of code originally
submitted by

Summary blurb:
The Mantel test is permutation test of correlation between two distance
matrices. It can also be used
to investigate partial correlation among 3 distance matrices or to test
spatial effects in designed
experiments.

Cheers,
Joel Reynolds
Joel H. Reynolds                                          Festina lente=>
                                                                    make
haste slowly
Joel_Reynolds at fishgame.state.ak.us
Biometrician
Gene Conservation Laboratory
Division of Commercial Fisheries, Alaska Department of Fish and Game
*************************************************************************
mantel.fcn _ function(m1,m2,nperm=1000,graph=F,rpt=F)
{
  # Mantel test for correlation between two matrices via permutation test
  # of the r, the standardized Mantel "z statistic".
  #####################################
  # m1, m2 - distance matrices whose correlation is to be tested
  # nperm - number of random permutations to use in approximating the
  #         matrix correlation reference distribution under the null
  #         hypothesis of no correlation.
  # graph - flag - graph reference distribution?
  # rpt - permutation progress indicator - if rpt is assigned an integer
  #       value, say rpt=100, then every 100th permutation will get
  #       noted on the screen.
  #
  # Output - list with following elements
  # Z - observed mantel statistic (sum of elementwise matrix product of
  #     m1 and m2, after diagonals of each have been set to 0.
  # R - standardized mantel statistic, i.e., the matrix correlation
coefficient.
  # UpperP - Estimated upper tail p-value from permutation test.
  # SymmetricZ - observed mantel statistic assuming symmetric matrices are
  #     given. SymmetricZ = Z/2
  ################
  # Note: This can be used to calculate partial correlations between
  # three distance matrices, Y1, Y2, and Y3 (each measured with error).
  # Assume you want correlation between Y1 and Y2 (holding Y3 fixed),
  # i.e. r_12.3, then:
  # 1. Regress Y1 on Y3 and get residual matrix, D1.3
  # 2. Regress Y2 on Y3 and get residual matrix, D2.3
  # 3. run mantel on D1.3, D2.3
  ################################################
  # REFS. Bryan F.J. Manly. 1986. "Multivariate statistical methods: a
primer". Chapman & Hall.
  #       Fortin, M. J., and Gurevitch, J.  1993.  Mantel Tests: Spatial
structure in field experiments.
  #            In "Design and Analysis of Ecological Experiments", edited by
S. Scheiner and J. Gurevitch.
  #            Chapman & Hall, pgs 342-359.
  # Partial Mantel tests & normalization:
  #       Smouse, P. E., Long, J. C., and Sokal, R. R. 1986. Multiple
Regression and
  #            Correlation Extensions of the Mantel test of matrix
correspondence.
  #            Systematic Zoology 35(4): 627-632.
  #
  # Extensions:
  #         Cheverud, J. M., G. P. Wagner, M. M. Dow.  1989.  Methods for
the comparative
  #           analysis of variation patterns.  Systematic Zoology 39:
201-213.
  #
  # Genetic Applications:
  #       Hutchison, D., and Templeton, A. R. 1999. Correlation of pairwise
genetic
  #           and geographic distance measures: inferring the relative
influences
  #           of gene flow and drift on the distribution of genetic
variability.
  #           Evolution 53(6): 1898-1914.
  ###################################
  # Original code by Ben Bolker <ben at eno.Princeton.EDU> 1997, Snews archive
  # Extended and revised by Joel H. Reynolds
joel_reynolds at fishgame.state.ak.us 9 March 2001
  # If you encounter problems with this code, please contact Joel Reynolds.
  # This code may be redistributed and freely used for non-commercial
purposes.
  ##################################################################

  if (!is.matrix(m1) || !is.matrix(m2)) return(cat("Data must be matrix
objects."))
  if (prod(dim(m1),dim(m2))!=(dim(m1)[1])^4) return(cat("Matrices must be
equal dimension and square."))

   n _ dim(m1)[1]
  # distance matrices - all diags should be zero
    m1_m1-diag(diag(m1))
    m2_m2-diag(diag(m2))

  # Calculate standardization components
    m1bar_sum(m1)/(n*(n-1)) # off-diagonal mean
    m2bar_sum(m2)/(n*(n-1)) # off-diagonal mean

    m1SS_sum(m1^2)-n*(n-1)*(m1bar^2)
    m2SS_sum(m2^2)-n*(n-1)*(m2bar^2)

 # Observed Mantel statistic - elementwise matrix product
    realz_sum(m1*m2)

 # Permutation test is invariant to standardization, so
 # conduct on raw Mantel statistic.
   enum_F
   if (nperm>gamma(n+1)) {
      cat("Warning: nperm>n!, enumerating permutations =",gamma(n+1),"\n")
      nperm _ gamma(n+1) #gamma(n+1)=n!
      enum_T
   }
   nullstats _ numeric(nperm)
   for (i in (1:nperm)) {
      if (enum)
         nullstats[i] _ sum(m1*perm.rowscols(m2,n,i))
      else
         nullstats[i] _ sum(m1*perm.rowscols(m2,n))
      if (is.numeric(rpt))
         if (i %% rpt == 0)
            cat(i,"...\n")
   }
   nullstats _ sort(nullstats)
   # upper tail p-value
   Upval _(sum(nullstats>=realz)+1)/(nperm+1)
    # correlation estimate.
    realr_(realz-n*(n-1)*m1bar*m2bar)/sqrt(m1SS*m2SS)

   if (graph) {
      # get standardize statistics
        nullrstats_(nullstats-n*(n-1)*m1bar*m2bar)/sqrt(m1SS*m2SS)
      plot(density(nullrstats),type="l",
       main="Approx. Null Dist. of Standardized Mantel statistic",
       xlab="r",ylab="# of permutations",
       sub=paste("Observed r=",round(realr,3),
        ": Upper Tail p-value=",round(Upval,4),":",nperm," permutations"))
      abline(v=realr,lty=2)
   }
   list(Z=realz, SymmetricZ=realz/2, R=realr,UpperP=Upval)
}

perm.rowscols _ function(m1,n,p=NULL)
{
# permute rows and columns of a matrix
# m1 is a (square) nxn matrix
# if p is numeric then the pth permutation will be taken, otherwise
#   a random permutation will be taken
   if (is.numeric(p))
     s _ gen.perm(p,n)
   else
     s _ sample(1:n)
   m1[s,s]
}

gen.perm _ function(k,n,vec=1:n)
{
#  generate a specified permutation of a vector
#  k is an integer between 1 and n!
#  n is the length of the sample
#  vec is the vector to permute, 1:n by default
   p _ as.list(vec)
   perm _ numeric(n)
   for (i in (n:1)) {
      v _ (k%%i)+1
      perm[i] _ p[[v]]
      p[[v]] _ NULL
      k _ k %/% i
   }
   perm
}

all.perm _ function(n,vec=1:n)
{
# generate all permutations of a list of length n
#  (1:n by default)
   k _ matrix(NA,nrow=gamma(n+1),ncol=n)
   for (i in (1:gamma(n+1)))
       k[i,] _ gen.perm(i,n,vec)
   k
}

############################################### Help Documentation ########

    Mantel test of correlation between two (distance) matrices

DESCRIPTION:

    The Mantel test is generally used to test correlation between two
distance
    matrices. For example, given geographic distance between populations
    and genetic distance between populations one may want to explore whether
    genetic distance is correlated with geographic distance (evidence of
dispersal,
    or straying).  Standard correlation tests are inappropriate due to the
dependence
    among entries in a distance matrix.  This is overcome by using a
permutation test.

USAGE:
    mantel.fcn(m1, m2, nperm=1000, graph=F, rpt=F)

REQUIRED ARGUMENTS:

m1
    first nxn distance matrix. It is assumed that distance between two
identical
    objects is zero, so diagonal entries should be zero (if they are not,
they will
    be set to zero). Need not be a symmetric matrix; i.e., dist(a,b) may
differ
    from dist(b,a).

m2
    second nxn distance matrix. Same requirements hold.

OPTIONAL ARGUMENTS:
nperm
    Number of permutations to use in approximating the correlation
statistic's
    reference distribution when there is no correlation (the null
hypothesis).

graph
    Logical flag - should a plot of the approximate permutation distribution
be made?
    The plot marks the observed value and lists the correlation and p-value
in the subtitle.

rpt
    F or an integer.  If an integer value is given (say rpt=100), then every
100 iterations
    of the permutation loop a progress notice will be printed to the screen.

VALUE:
    mantel.fcn() returns a list with four elements: Z - the Mantel
statistic,
    SymmetricZ - the Mantel statistic for symmetric matrices (only
calculated from
    lower triangular submatrices),
    r - the standardized Mantel correlation coefficient, and
    UpperP - the upper tail p-value = Prob(Random Corr >= Obs. Corr).
    These values are also printed to the screen and, if graph=T, on the
subtitle of the graph.

DETAILS:
    The original Mantel statistic, Z, is the sum of the elementwise matrix
product
    (Hadamard product), with the diagonal elements removed. Most
descriptions of the test
    assume distance matrices (so diagonals are zero and matrices are
symmetric), though
    mantel.fcn actually calculates the statistic over all non-diagnonal
elements, allowing
    for non-symmetric distance measures (M1[i,j] != M1[j,i]). As derived in
Smouse, Long,
    and Sokal (1986), the Mantel statistic is basically a product-moment
correlation coefficient.
    Though not included in the current code, the general idea can be
extended to rank
    correlation.

    The Mantel test has been extended to investigate partial correlation
(Smouse et al. 1986),
    spatial influences on designed experiments (Fortin and Gurevitch 1993),
among others.

REFERENCES:
    Bryan F.J. Manly. 1986. "Multivariate statistical methods: a primer".
Chapman & Hall.

    Fortin, M. J., and Gurevitch, J.  1993.  Mantel Tests: Spatial structure
in field
        experiments. In "Design and Analysis of Ecological Experiments",
edited by S.
        Scheiner and J. Gurevitch. Chapman & Hall, pgs 342-359.

    Sokal, R. R., Rohlf, F. J. 1995. Biometry (3rd edition).  W. H. Freeman
& Co.
        pgs 813-819.

Partial Mantel tests & normalization:
   Smouse, P. E., Long, J. C., and Sokal, R. R. 1986. Multiple Regression
and
        Correlation Extensions of the Mantel test of matrix correspondence.
        Systematic Zoology 35(4): 627-632.

Genetic Applications:
    Hutchison, D., and Templeton, A. R. 1999. Correlation of pairwise
genetic
        and geographic distance measures: inferring the relative influences
        of gene flow and drift on the distribution of genetic variability.
        Evolution 53(6): 1898-1914.

    Sokal, R. R., Smouse, P. E., and Neel, J. V. 1986.  The genetic
structure of a
        tribal population, the Yanomama Indians.  XV. Patterns inferred by
        autocorrelation analysis.  Genetics 114: 259-287.

NOTE
    Original code by Ben Bolker <ben at eno.Princeton.EDU> 1997, Snews archive
    Extended and revised by Joel H. Reynolds
joel_reynolds at fishgame.state.ak.us 2001

EXAMPLES:
# From Manly (1986)
# Penrose distances between 5 male Egyptian skulls from various epochs.
> PenDist
      [,1]  [,2]  [,3]  [,4]  [,5]
[1,] 0.000 0.023 0.216 0.493 0.736
[2,] 0.023 0.000 0.163 0.404 0.583
[3,] 0.216 0.163 0.000 0.108 0.244
[4,] 0.493 0.404 0.108 0.000 0.066
[5,] 0.736 0.583 0.244 0.066 0.000
# Time between epochs during which the skulls originated.
> TimeDist
     [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4] [,5]
[1,] 0.00 0.70 2.15 3.80 4.15
[2,] 0.70 0.00 1.45 3.10 3.45
[3,] 2.15 1.45 0.00 1.65 2.00
[4,] 3.80 3.10 1.65 0.00 0.35
[5,] 4.15 3.45 2.00 0.35 0.00
> mantel.fcn(PenDist,TimeDist,graph=F)
